{"id":66348397466392,"type":"album","name":"New York","artist":"Lou Reed","year":1989,"weight":22,"info":{"cover":"https://dn711108.ca.archive.org/0/items/mbid-877f9d7a-a1ea-464a-a8a1-a851ab1046c1/mbid-877f9d7a-a1ea-464a-a8a1-a851ab1046c1-18649338042.jpg","kind":"Album","tracks":14,"country":"XE","scaruffi":8.0,"yt":"9PFH6DYictM","links":{"spotify":"https://open.spotify.com/album/7xGqf9DddW89cg7m6zX88t","youtube":"https://music.youtube.com/playlist?list=OLAK5uy_ldWQypIgsqYg7sReS8d26X_pptbbTMhZw","rateyourmusic":"https://rateyourmusic.com/release/album/lou-reed/new-york"},"desc":"New York is the fifteenth solo studio album by American musician Lou Reed, released in January 1989 by Sire Records. The album received universal critical acclaim upon release, and is widely considered to be among Reed's strongest solo efforts. It is highly regarded for the strength and force of its lyrical content; Reed stated that he required simple music so that it would not distract from his frank lyrics. The single \"Dirty Blvd.\" was a number-one hit on the newly created Billboard Modern Rock Tracks chart for four weeks. Reed's former band, the Velvet Underground, were at the peak of ."},"artist_ref":{"id":222115320560673,"name":"Lou Reed","info":{"img":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Lou_Reed_at_the_Hop_Farm_Music_Festival.jpg?width=500"}},"labels":[],"genres":[{"id":53681850040905,"type":"genre","name":"Rock & Roll","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1066,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":109063683126539,"type":"genre","name":"Hard Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":562,"yt":null,"src":"musthear"},{"id":191826593279813,"type":"genre","name":"Art Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":531,"yt":null,"src":"musthear,rym"}],"lists":[{"id":52032266791182,"name":"Scaruffi 1980s","src":"musthear","grp":null,"list_year":null,"rank":70,"year":null,"note":null,"total":877,"scaruffi":8.0},{"id":208356503087556,"name":"RYM Top — Rock — Art Rock","src":"musthear","grp":"rateyourmusic","list_year":null,"rank":127,"year":null,"note":null,"total":200,"scaruffi":8.0}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":null,"body":"Lou Reed's 1989 album \"New York\" included detailed liner notes that specified its recording at Media Sound Studio B in New York City, and his intent for it to be heard in a single 58-minute sitting \"as though it were a book or a movie.\" Reed explicitly stated his guitar panning (\"I'm on the left, and the other guitarist, Mike Rathke, is on the right\") and credited Rathke for two clean solos on \"Endless Cycle\" and \"Sick of You,\" before thanking all involved with the guitar equipment. \"Lou Reed from 1989.\"","year":1989,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"Digital Debris  Part 3: Liner Notes","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KaIp6KVn-co"}]}],"book_tracks":[{"id":66348397466392,"artist":"Lou Reed","artist_id":222115320560673,"title":"New York","year":1989,"ess":1,"yt":"9PFH6DYictM","kind":"a","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Decadencia y glam-rock","scene_id":67907700002938,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#glam","chapters":["Sección 4.4"],"pages":[[1,89,null]]}],"others":[{"id":70371387639706,"type":"album","name":"Metal Machine Music","year":1975,"weight":67},{"id":142902714616731,"type":"album","name":"Transformer","year":1972,"weight":51},{"id":132223116171397,"type":"album","name":"Berlin","year":1973,"weight":42},{"id":199271174054410,"type":"song","name":"Walk on the Wild Side","year":1972,"weight":28},{"id":237259393171384,"type":"song","name":"Vicious","year":1972,"weight":13},{"id":67508209434837,"type":"album","name":"Street Hassle","year":1978,"weight":12},{"id":211016329439805,"type":"album","name":"Legendary Hearts","year":1983,"weight":11},{"id":130737084915293,"type":"album","name":"Blue Mask","year":1982,"weight":11},{"id":280